From: dresden196 <50474030+dresden196@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] NickelOS: the desktop, icons for ~/Desktop

What is in the Desktop folder of the home folder (My files) shows on the
desktop, under every window: one icon per file or folder, in Files' icon
style, on a grid in the primary display's work area (docs/desktop.md in
overlay-nickel). It lives in Ash (ash/nickel/desktop): a widget in the
wallpaper container, kept above the wallpaper, shown in an active session
in clamshell mode outside overview.

- Icons sit on a tile that follows the light or dark theme. Click selects
  (Ctrl adds), double-click opens (folders in Files, files with Files'
  default app); with touch or a pen, tap and double tap, a long press (or
  a two-finger tap) opens the menu, and dragging moves icons (moving on
  after a long press closes the menu, as on the shelf). A touch whose end
  the widget never sees (a menu takes it) does not leave its view as the
  widget's gesture handler: the next touch goes to the view under it. Right-click on an icon: Open, Show in Files,
  Rename, Move to trash. Right-click on the desktop: New folder, Open
  Desktop in Files, then the wallpaper's usual menu (wallpaper, shelf).
- Dragging icons moves them to other cells; each item's cell is kept in the
  profile pref nickel.desktop.positions, by name. Dragging them out gives
  their files to Files and Linux apps. A mouse drag shows the item itself
  (tile, glyph, name) under the pointer, held where it was picked up.
- Files dropped from Files are moved there from My files (as Files does)
  and copied otherwise; from Linux apps, copied. They land where dropped.
- Renaming (and a new folder's name) is asked in a bubble at the icon.

Chrome's part, NickelDesktopClientImpl (ash::NickelDesktopClient), gives the
desktop folder and does what needs the profile: opening, Show in Files,
Move to trash and copying or moving through Files' I/O tasks.

